{
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Chain Rule starts with one simple question: if the input doubles, what should happen to the output?\n\nFor identical items at the same rate, twice the quantity means twice the cost. Half the quantity means half the cost.\n\nThat same scaling idea powers the whole chapter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose 6 identical notebooks cost ₹90.\n\nA notebook has not changed price, so the cost per notebook is fixed. Going from 6 notebooks to 10 multiplies the quantity by 10/6. The cost must be multiplied by the same factor.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A fast mental route is unit rate: ₹90 ÷ 6 = ₹15 each.\nThen 10 notebooks cost 10 × ₹15 = ₹150.\n\nThe reason this works is direct proportion: quantity and cost move together.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
