  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Direct Proportion: Scale the Same Way",
    "description": "Scale cost, weight, quantity, distance, and output using one common factor.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with a price-tag idea: twice as many identical items cost twice as much. Turn that into a scale-factor habit rather than a cross-multiplication ritual. Progress through unit-rate thinking, dozens, mixed units, weight-by-length, partial work, bundle scaling and all-direct multi-factor output. Each question asks the learner to predict the direction first, then calculate with the smallest clean factor.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 15, Chain Rule. Approved scope: Teach direct proportion as a constant unit rate: if one quantity is multiplied by a factor, the dependent quantity is multiplied by the same factor. Build from cost per item and weight per length to dozens, mixed units, partial-work scaling, bundled quantities, and multi-factor cases where every factor is direct. Emphasize finding a clean scale factor before multiplying and checking that the answer moves in the expected direction. Prepare learners for the chapter's basic cost, weight, production, recipe and same-rate objective patterns without copying source questions."
  },
  "scriptId": "cr-01-direct-proportion-unit-scaling",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "schemaVersion": 1
